===Etymology 1===
{{PIE word|en|h₂éd}}{{root|en|ine-pro|*weyd-}}
The {{glossary|verb}} is an {{aphetic form|en|atwite|t=(''obsolete'') to blame, reproach|nocap=1}},<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=1165072605|date=September 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|alt=twit{{sup|2}}|pos=v}}</ref> from {{inh|en|enm|atwiten|t=to attribute (something) to someone; to blame (something) on someone; to accuse or charge (someone) with something; to speak ill of; to taunt}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=atwīten|pos=v|id=MED2966}}</ref> from {{inh|en|ang|ætwītan|t=to blame, reproach; to censure, upbraid; to taunt}}, from {{m|ang|æt-|pos={{glossary|prefix}} meaning ‘at, near; toward’}} + {{m|ang|wītan|t=to accuse; to blame, reproach}} (from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wītaną|t=to punish; to torment; to know; to see}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*weyd-|t=to see}}).

The {{glossary|noun}} is probably derived from the verb, although it is attested in print earlier.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.''{{sup|1}}|noformat=1|id=5654050852|date=July 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|alt=twit{{sup|1}}|pos=n}}</ref>

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## To [[blame#Verb|blame]] or [[reproach#Verb|reproach]] (someone), especially in a [[good-natured]] or [[teasing#Adjective|teasing]] [[manner#Noun|manner]]; also, to [[ridicule#Verb|ridicule]] or [[tease#Verb|tease]] (someone).
##: {{sense|blame}} {{synonyms|en|censure|upbraid|Thesaurus:blame}}
##: {{sense|ridicule}} {{synonyms|en|taunt|Thesaurus:deride}}
##* {{RQ:Udall Ralph Roister Doister|page=36|passage=No man for deſpite, / By worde or by write / His felowe to '''twite''', {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|act=III|scene=i|page=131|column=2|passage=Hath he not '''tvvit''' our Soueraigne Lady here / VVith ignominious vvords, though Clarkely coucht? / As if ſhe had ſuborned ſome to ſvveare / Falſe allegations, to o'rethrovv his ſtate.}}
##* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables of Aesop|section=Preface|page=12|passage=Hovv much are vve Oblig'd then, {{...}} to ''[[w:Aesop|Æſop]]'', in the Firſt Place, as the ''Founder'', and ''Original Author'', or ''Inventer'' of This Art of Schooling Mankind into Better Manners; by Minding Men of their Errors vvithout '''Tvvitting''' them for what's Amiſs, and by That Means Flaſhing the Light of their Ovvn Conſciences in their Ovvn Faces!}}
##* {{RQ:Tillotson Works|page=101|passage=Novv this, 'tis probable, theſe ''Scoffers'' '''tvvitted''' the Chriſtians vvithall; And becauſe [[w:Jesus|Chriſt]] did not come vvhen ſome looked for him, they concluded he vvould not come at all.}}
##* {{RQ:Baillie Dramas|volume=I|act=III|scene=ii|page=55|passage=Nay, do not '''twit''' me now with all the freaks, / And levities, and gambols charged upon me / By every lean-faced dame that wears a hood.}}
##* {{RQ:Trollope Belton Estate|volume=I|chapter=Captain Aylmer Meets His Constituents|page=193|passage=Anything would be better than being '''twitted''' in this way. How can I help it that I am not a man and able to work for my bread?}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1887|author=New Zealand. Parliament|title=Parliamentary Debates|page=499|passage=The honourable member for Ellesmere, on the opening of this session, got up and '''twitted''' them with not being there on the first day.}}
##* '''1955''', [[w:Rex Stout|Rex Stout]], "When a Man Murders...",<!--... in title--> in [[w:Three Witnesses (book)|''Three Witnesses'']], October 1994 [[w:Bantam Books|Bantam]] edition, {{ISBN|0553249592}}, page 106:
##*: Mr. Cramer, a policeman, came this morning and '''twitted''' me for having let a murderer hoodwink me.
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1962|month=August|title=Talking of Trains: Under their hats|journal=Modern Railways|page=80|passage=Secrecy about B.R. plans for reorganisation and closure of lines and notably some failures to consult with staff representatives concerned with redundancy, are defects with which the railway unions have '''twitted''' Dr. Beeching.}}
##* '''2007''', Bernard Porter, "Did He Puff his Crimes to Please a Bloodthirsty Readership?", review of ''Stanley: The Impossible Life of Africa’s Greatest Explorer'' by Tim Jeal, ''London Review of Books'', 5 April, 29:7, p. 10
##*: H. R. Fox Bourne, secretary of the Aborigines' Protection Society – often '''twitted''' for being an ‘armchair critic’ – wrote in a review of one of Stanley's books {{...}}
## {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[criticize]] or [[disapprove]] of (something), especially in a good-natured or teasing manner.
##: {{synonyms|en|censure|ridicule}}
## {{lb|en|computing}} To [[ignore]] or [[kill file#Verb|kill file]] (a [[user]] on a [[bulletin board system]]).
##* {{quote-newsgroup|en|year=1995|author=Michelle Jackson|title=Debutante/Question about Tori Shirts|newsgroup=rec.music.tori-amos|passage=However, on the Internet BBS's such as Quartz (now dead), Prism, Monsoon, Sunset, ect{{SIC}}, someone pulling that kind of crap is likely to get flamed quite fast and '''twitted''' before he/she can breathe.}}
##* {{quote-newsgroup|en|year=2002|author=Chris Hoppman|title=FidoNet Feed Needed|newsgroup=alt.bbs|passage=And no, there is no "thought purification program" that can filter out some folks{{sic}} obscene ideas that can be expressed w/o written vulgarities. That has to be simply "dealt" with, either by ignoring or '''twitting''' the individual that offends habitually.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}} ''Followed by'' '''[[it#Pronoun|it]]''': to [[speak#Verb|speak]] or [[write#Verb|write]] (something) in a [[taunting#Adjective|taunting]] or teasing [[manner#Noun|manner]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive}}
## To blame or reproach, especially in a good-natured or teasing manner.
## {{lb|en|obsolete|except|Britain|dialectal}} To be [[indiscreet]]; to [[gossip#Verb|gossip]].

