#* {{RQ:Strype Ecclesiastical Memorials|volume=I|part=Appendix|chapter=Numb[er] XVII. The Confession of John Tyball a Lollard; Charges with Heresy.|year='''1528''' May 8 (date written; Gregorian calendar)|page=38|passage=[T]he ſayd ''Thomas Hilles'' & this Reſpondent ſhevvyd the Frear ''Barons'' of certayne old Bookes that they had: as of iiij Evangeliſtes, and certayne Epiſtles of ''Peter'' & ''Poule'' in ''Engliſhe''. VVhich Bookes the ſayd Frear dyd litle regard, and made a '''tvvyte''' of it, & ſayd, A poynt for them, for they be not to be regarded tovvard the nevv printed Teſtament in ''Engliſhe''.}}
#* {{RQ:Etherege Comical Revenge|scene=v|page=89|passage=''Wid''[''ow'']. Novv I have receiv'd you into my Family, / I hop you vvill let my maids go quietly about / Their buſineſs, Sir. / ''S''[''ir''] ''Fred''[''erick Frollick'']. Upon condition there be no '''tvvits''' of the good man / Departed; no preſcription pleaded for evil cuſtoms / On the VVedding night.}}
#* {{RQ:Hunt Men Women|volume=II|chapter=Lady Mary Wortley Montagu|page=224|passage=[S]he, beginning to despair of finally winning him, looked about for other consolations, not, however, without an occasional '''twit''' at him for disappointing her.}}
# {{senseid|en|fool}}{{lb|en|informal}} An [[annoying#Adjective|annoying]] or [[foolish]] [[person#Noun|person]].
#: {{synonyms|en|Thesaurus:fool}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Larry Kramer|title=[[w:Just Say No (play)|Just Say No: A Play about a Farce]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:St. Martin's Press|St. Martin’s Press]]|year='''1988''' (date written)|year_published=1989|section=Act II|page=66|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/justsaynoplayabo00kram_0/page/66/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-312-03371-2|passage=What do you mean, since when did I become such a radical fairy! {{...}} Since I started knowing '''twits''' like you, you '''twit'''!}}
# {{lb|en|British|dialectal|archaic}} A person who [[chatter#Verb|chatters]] or [[gossip#Verb|gossips]] [[inanely]]; a [[chatterer]], a [[gossip#Noun|gossip]] or [[gossiper]]; also, a person who [[divulge]]s [[private#Adjective|private]] [[information]] about others or is [[indiscreet]]; a [[tattletale#Noun|tattletale]].
#: {{synonyms|en|Thesaurus:gossiper}}
#* {{RQ:d'Urfey Wit and Mirth|volume=VI|chapter=A Song. Set by Mr. {{w|Henry Purcell}}.|page=241|passage=Young ''Strephon'' he has Woo'd me long, / And Courted me with Pipe and Song; / But I a silly, silly peevish '''Twit''', / For want of Sense, for want of Wit, / Have phoo'd, and cry'd, / Have pish'd, and fy'd, / And play'd the fool, and lost my Time, / And almost slipp'd, and almost slipp'd, / And almost slipp'd my Maiden Prime.}}

